#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106886} :activity/id #uuid "55efdebf-c5f4-5896-8a96-24cfe4c710eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049883 :topic/id #uuid "1adf18d4-239e-50f8-b7e4-88f56c72baad" :topic/math-academy-id 1264} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107153 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNow, if a surface is given by the explicit equation $z=g(x,y)$ over a plane region $D$ in Cartesian coordinates, then the canonical parametrization of this surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\langle x,\\:  y, \\: g(x,y) \\rangle, \\qquad (x,y) \\in D.\n$$\n\nMoreover, we know that under the canonical parametrization, the magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'}‖ & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "411ccf43-163d-53bb-b1f4-2b6832c905e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7650 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6f07960b-27be-5aa0-93a1-7182414f7f51" :step/math-academy-id 44418 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example